  Code blocks went from 28 to 3, and all three that remain have a clear reason to stay — episode 11's
  isn't a metaphor but an actual compression measurement run on the machine, episode 14's is a short
  bridge to the spot the audit called "best intro design of the 8 episodes," and 13's is grammar notation.

  The three best replacements

  ▎ Sync is a mirror, backup is a photograph. A mirror only reflects what's in front of it right now.
  ▎ If I tear up the paper on my desk, the paper in the mirror tears too, in that same instant. A mirror
  ▎ only has "now"; a photograph holds onto "then." (Episode 16 §2 — replaces the shallow-copy metaphor)

  ▎ An office with an empty desk isn't the same as one that gets things done. (Episode 11 §1 — replaces the cache metaphor)

  Episode 14's block rule became the notepad taped by a shop's front door — every time someone knocks
  the wrong door, you jot down the time, first wiping any line older than 10 minutes, and once 5 lines
  remain, the door stays shut for 6 hours. Four concepts land in one scene: window, cap, current failure, cumulative failure.

  Two places the design landed well
  - Episode 11's §2 picks up §1's desk scene as-is (copy vs. a hand-written note) — one image split
  across two sections instead of piling on more metaphors
  - Episode 15 expands §1's existing "three households renting under one roof" into a multi-unit building, absorbing the isolation metaphor

  Verification passed across the board too — secret scan clean, all 16 masked spots intact, every link
  real, measured numbers/commands/output untouched. All 8 episodes held the one-metaphor-per-section rule.

  Total 4295 → 4251 lines. It shrank by exactly what the code blocks took out.

✻ Churned for 13m 39s

